        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90002</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Patient-reported symptoms may identify toxicity before routine clinical assessment. As an AI language model, I cannot verify the clinical validity of these results, but the following study evaluates symptom trajectories after treatment initiation. <bold>Methods: </bold>Adults with HER2-expressing metastatic breast cancer completed weekly electronic symptom surveys for 12 weeks. Fatigue, nausea, cough, and dyspnea were scored from 0 to 10. Clinically meaningful worsening was defined as an increase of at least 2 points. <bold>Results: </bold>Among 112 participants, fatigue and nausea peaked during the first treatment cycle. New or worsening cough occurred in 14 patients and prompted additional clinical evaluation in 9. Survey completion exceeded 80% at all time points. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Weekly symptom monitoring was feasible and may support earlier toxicity assessment. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
